---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-japan`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in Japan',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in Japan', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in Japan: Fish, Sun Avoidance, and a Surprising Deficiency Rate"
  description="Japan eats a lot of vitamin D-rich fish, yet deficiency is widespread — driven by strong cultural sun avoidance and skin-whitening habits. The paradox, and what the data show."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-japan", label: "Vitamin D in Japan" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in Japan</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Japan is a fascinating case study. It has one of the highest fish intakes in the world — salmon,
      mackerel, sardines, and dried shiitake are dietary staples, and these are among the few genuinely
      rich food sources of vitamin D. And yet large studies keep finding that deficiency is remarkably
      common. The explanation lies less in the diet than in a deep cultural preference for pale skin and
      avoiding the sun.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The sun-avoidance factor</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      In Japan, fair skin is strongly valued, and sun protection is a year-round habit for many —
      parasols (higasa), long sleeves, gloves, hats, and heavy use of high-SPF sunscreen and
      "whitening" cosmetics. This dramatically reduces cutaneous vitamin D synthesis even during the
      warm months. Combined with a latitude range of roughly 24°N (Okinawa) to 45°N (Hokkaido), the
      northern half of the country also has a real winter with limited UVB, layering a seasonal deficit
      on top of the behavioural one.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the data show</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A large 2023 analysis of over 5,000 Tokyo residents made headlines by reporting that the vast
      majority had 25(OH)D below 30 ng/mL, with a large share below 20 ng/mL — levels lowest in spring,
      as expected after winter. Studies in young women, in particular, repeatedly show high deficiency
      rates, consistent with the strongest sun-avoidance behaviour in that group. The fish-rich diet
      raises the floor but does not, on its own, compensate for near-total avoidance of sun.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Diet, fortification, and dosing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Japan does not have the milk-fortification programs common in North America, so intake comes from
      natural foods. The Japanese dietary reference intake for vitamin D was revised upward in recent
      years (to around 8.5 µg / 340 IU per day for adults as an adequate intake), reflecting the
      recognition that levels are low. For those who avoid the sun and eat little fish, a modest daily
      supplement is a reasonable way to close the gap. Dried mushrooms (sun-dried shiitake) are a useful
      plant source — see our <a href="/vitamin-d-foods" class="text-amber-700 underline">foods page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The takeaway</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Japan is a clean illustration that <em>behaviour beats latitude and even diet</em> when it comes
      to vitamin D. A country that eats plenty of the right fish can still be widely deficient if it
      systematically keeps the sun off the skin. For most people the answer is not to abandon sun
      protection — Japan's approach protects against skin ageing and cancer — but to lean on diet and a
      supplement instead.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> If you consistently avoid the sun, consider asking about a
      25(OH)D test. A sensible dietary and supplement strategy is safer than deliberately increasing sun
      exposure.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-japan" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
